      "rationale": "Comparison is well served, but the measuring half of this competency survives in only two items, both asking how long the object is. Eight of the ten samples hand the pupil two finished numbers, which is number comparison and needs no measuring tool at all."
    },
    "comprehensive_coverage": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Longer and shorter are both asked, and centimetres and metres both appear in the comparison items, yet the actual measuring items only ever request an answer in cm. Measuring a length in metres with a tool is never generated."
    },
    "cognitive_capacity": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "One-step comparisons of two stated lengths, phrased in a single short sentence with familiar school objects, are well within what a Grade 2 Quarter 2 pupil can hold."
    },
    "variant_comprehensiveness": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "Three formatters are used but only two situations exist: two objects side by side, or two distances from a landmark. A measuring item where the pupil reads a scale appears just twice and with word-for-word identical wording both times."
    },
    "competency_alignment": {
      "verdict": "CONCERN",
      "rationale": "The step up in the later seeds is purely numeric, moving from a pencil at 10 and a ruler at 15 to distances of 82 and 37. Comparing larger integers is a number-sense axis, not a measurement one, so the added difficulty does not build this competency."
    },
